<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huelva</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Onoba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Huelva is a port city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>, south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odiel">Odiel</a> and Tinto rivers meet, and capital of the province of the same name. As ancient Onoba it was a major centre of the Tartessian world, where Phoenician and Greek traders obtained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> from the mines of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-tinto">Río Tinto</a> area. The province, bordering Portugal, remains a mining and farming region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huelva.jpg" alt="Huelva" width="320" height="164" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jose A. · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Huelva_(Spain)_(41302627111)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
